## Serve graph and GraphQL libraries from the app instead of public CDNs

Context:
A customer network blocks d3js.org. The ontology map then failed with
"ReferenceError: d3 is not defined" while Bootstrap (cdn.jsdelivr.net) still
loaded. The knowledge-graph explorer (cdnjs.cloudflare.com) and the GraphQL
view (unpkg.com) depend on equally blockable hosts. These libraries now ship
with the app, pinned to the versions the app already used.

Changes:
1. src/front/static/global/vendor/
   New, unmodified upstream bundles: d3 7.9.0, graphology 0.26.0,
   graphology-library 0.8.0, sigma 3.0.2, graphiql 3.9.0 (JS + CSS),
   react 18.3.1, react-dom 18.3.1.
2. src/front/templates/partials/ontology/_ontology_map.html,
   src/front/templates/partials/mapping/_mapping_design.html
   Load D3 through url_for('static', ...) instead of d3js.org.
3. src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-swrl.js,
   src/front/static/query/js/query-ontology-viewer.js,
   src/front/static/registry/js/registry.js,
   src/front/static/query/js/query-sigmagraph.js,
   src/front/static/query/js/query-graphql.js
   Lazy loaders point at /static/global/vendor/... paths.
4. licenses/README.md
   Pin D3, GraphiQL and React versions and note the vendored location.
5. tests/units/front/test_vendored_graph_libs.py
   New: no front source uses d3js.org / cdnjs / unpkg, no graph library
   comes from jsdelivr, and every vendor reference exists on disk.
6. tests/units/front/test_i18n.py
   The script-string scan skips static/global/vendor (third-party code).

